se project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain and reproduce.","example":"Release evidence:\n- acceptance checks pass\n- migration and rollback tested\n- offline install documented\n- known limits published\n- next improvement prioritized","practice":"Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.","plans":[{"id":"python-m16-l1","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using capstone delivery and maintenance with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."},{"id":"python-m16-l2","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to capstone delivery and maintenance against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]}],"projects":[{"id":"python-p1","title":"Household expense ledger","brief":"Help a fictional family understand weekly spending in AFN.","hours":6,"requirements":["Add dated expenses in integer AFN with a category.","Reject negative amounts and blank categories.","Produce category totals and a weekly total from local JSON."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"python-p2","title":"School attendance analyst","brief":"Help a teacher summarize attendance from a local CSV file.","hours":12,"requirements":["Validate student IDs, dates, and duplicate rows.","Calculate attendance percentages with a defined zero-session rule.","Export a UTF-8 report and test malformed CSV cases."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"python-p3","title":"Community shop inventory","brief":"Build an offline stock and sales tool for a small stationery shop.","hours":24,"requirements":["Persist items and sales in SQLite; use an atomic sale transaction.","Prevent overselling and retain stock after restart.","Provide low-stock reports, JSON export, and automated regression tests."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Program: a sequence of instructions a computer follows.","String: text enclosed in quotes.","Variable: a name that refers to a value.","Output: the result a program displays."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nNo programming experience is needed. Open Code lab. Replace its sample with the two lines below. Type straight quotes, not decorative quotation marks. Press Run code. You can also trace the example on paper.","code":"name = \"Salam\"\nprint(name)","trace":["Line 1 stores the string Salam under the name name. The equals sign assigns a value; it does not ask a question.","Line 2 reads that value and displays Salam. The quotes and variable name are not printed.","Change Salam to Kabul and run again. Only the displayed word changes."],"question":"What will print(name) display after name = \"Salam\"?","options":["Salam","name","Nothing"],"answer":0,"task":"Store Kabul in a variable named city, then display city on one line.","hint":"Replace both uses of name with city. Keep the text inside quotes.","solution":"city = \"Kabul\"\nprint(city)","result":"Kabul"}},{"id":"web-design","title":"Web Design","prerequisites":[],"tools":"Use a local text editor and browser. Open index.html directly; bundle CSS, images, and fonts locally. No CDN, online design service, or package manager is required.","modules":[{"title":"Semantic HTML","explanation":"A page is a document before it is a visual composition. Landmarks, headings, lists, and labels express relationships to browsers and assistive tools. Choose elements by meaning, then style them.","example":"<main>\n  <h1>Community library</h1>\n  <nav aria-label=\"Sections\"><a href=\"#hours\">Opening hours</a></nav>\n  <section id=\"hours\"><h2>Hours</h2><p>Saturday–Thursday, 9–4</p></section>\n</main>","practice":"a library page with a meaningful heading hierarchy and working internal navigation.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m1-l1","title":"Semantic HTML: guided investigation","level":"remember","minutes":60,"objective":"Identify the key terms and syntax in semantic html without consulting the example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a library page with a meaningful heading hierarchy and working internal navigation. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."},{"id":"web-design-m1-l2","title":"Semantic HTML: independent studio","level":"understand","minutes":90,"objective":"Explain how semantic html changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"a library page with a meaningful heading hierarchy and working internal navigation. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}]},{"title":"CSS cascade and typography","explanation":"The cascade resolves competing declarations; inheritance passes selected values to descendants. Use a small spacing and color system instead of unrelated numbers. Relative units support larger text settings.","example":"body { font: 1rem/1.6 system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; }\nmain { max-width: 65ch; margin-inline: auto; padding: 1.5rem; }\na:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #175c40; }","practice":"a readable type and spacing system with visible keyboard focus.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m2-l1","title":"CSS cascade and typography: guided investigation","level":"understand","minutes":60,"objective":"Explain how css cascade and typography changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a readable type and spacing system with visible keyboard focus. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."},{"id":"web-design-m2-l2","title":"CSS cascade and typography: independent studio","level":"apply","minutes":90,"objective":"Implement a readable type and spacing system with visible keyboard focus.","activity":"a readable type and spacing system with visible keyboard focus. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}]},{"title":"Responsive layout","explanation":"Flexbox aligns a row or column; Grid coordinates two dimensions. Let content choose breakpoints. Avoid fixed heights that clip translated or enlarged text, and test the narrowest supported viewport.","example":".cards { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr)); }","practice":"a card layout that reflows at 320, 768, and 1280 CSS pixels without horizontal overflow.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m3-l1","title":"Responsive layout: guided investigation","level":"apply","minutes":60,"objective":"Implement a card layout that reflows at 320, 768, and 1280 CSS pixels without horizontal overflow.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a card layout that reflows at 320, 768, and 1280 CSS pixels without horizontal overflow. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."},{"id":"web-design-m3-l2","title":"Responsive layout: independent studio","level":"analyze","minutes":90,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in responsive layout and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"a card layout that reflows at 320, 768, and 1280 CSS pixels without horizontal overflow. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}]},{"title":"Forms and accessibility","explanation":"Labels identify fields; instructions and error messages explain how to recover. Native controls provide useful keyboard behavior. Do not use color alone to indicate an error. HTML validation helps users but is not server-side security.","example":"<label for=\"name\">Student name</label>\n<input id=\"name\" name=\"name\" required aria-describedby=\"name-help\">\n<p id=\"name-help\">Enter the name used on your school record.</p>","practice":"a registration form with labels, keyboard access, and understandable required-field errors.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m4-l1","title":"Forms and accessibility: guided investigation","level":"analyze","minutes":60,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in forms and accessibility and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a registration form with labels, keyboard access, and understandable required-field errors. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."},{"id":"web-design-m4-l2","title":"Forms and accessibility: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to forms and accessibility against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a registration form with labels, keyboard access, and understandable required-field errors. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"RTL and inclusive design","explanation":"Set direction on the relevant document or region. Logical properties such as margin-inline adapt to direction; source-code blocks remain left-to-right. Test mixed numbers, names, and long translations with native readers before release.","example":"<article lang=\"fa\" dir=\"rtl\"><h1>کتابخانه</h1><p>ساعت کار: ۹ تا ۴</p></article>\n<pre dir=\"ltr\">print(\"Salam\")</pre>","practice":"an English and Dari page pair with logical spacing and a documented translation review checklist.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m5-l1","title":"RTL and inclusive design: guided investigation","level":"evaluate","minutes":60,"objective":"Judge two approaches to rtl and inclusive design against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. an English and Dari page pair with logical spacing and a documented translation review checklist. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."},{"id":"web-design-m5-l2","title":"RTL and inclusive design: independent studio","level":"create","minutes":90,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using rtl and inclusive design with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"an English and Dari page pair with logical spacing and a documented translation review checklist. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}]},{"title":"Design systems and usability","explanation":"Reusable tokens and components reduce accidental variation. Evaluate a design through tasks: can a learner locate hours, navigate with a keyboard, and read at 200% zoom? Record observed difficulties and revise one component at a time.","example":":root { --space: 1rem; --ink: #162f28; --surface: #fffdf5; }\n.card { padding: var(--space); color: var(--ink); background: var(--surface); }","practice":"a small component guide and a usability report with three observed issues and fixes.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m6-l1","title":"Design systems and usability: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using design systems and usability with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a small component guide and a usability report with three observed issues and fixes. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."},{"id":"web-design-m6-l2","title":"Design systems and usability: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to design systems and usability against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a small component guide and a usability report with three observed issues and fixes. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Design systems and component architecture","explanation":"This web design module teaches you to turn tokens, components, states, and content rules into a maintainable interface system. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain and reproduce.","example":":root { --space-2: .5rem; --brand: #087c89; }\n.button { padding: var(--space-2) 1rem; }\n.button:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid currentColor; }","practice":"Create a documented local design system with accessible default, hover, focus, error, and disabled states. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m7-l1","title":"Design systems and component architecture: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using design systems and component architecture with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. Create a documented local design system with accessible default, hover, focus, error, and disabled states. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."},{"id":"web-design-m7-l2","title":"Design systems and component architecture: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to design systems and component architecture against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Create a documented local design system with accessible default, hover, focus, error, and disabled states. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Advanced accessibility and performance","explanation":"This web design module teaches you to audit semantics, keyboard flow, contrast, motion, responsive images, and rendering cost under real constraints. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain and reproduce.","example":"@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  *, *::before, *::after { animation-duration: .01ms !important; }\n}","practice":"Audit and repair a multi-page site for keyboard, screen-reader, large-text, reduced-motion, and offline performance. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.","plans":[{"id":"web-design-m8-l1","title":"Advanced accessibility and performance: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using advanced accessibili