ords, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.","plans":[{"id":"web-development-m16-l1","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using capstone delivery and maintenance with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."},{"id":"web-development-m16-l2","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to capstone delivery and maintenance against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]}],"projects":[{"id":"web-development-p1","title":"Local event registration","brief":"Manage seats for a fictional workshop on one local computer.","hours":6,"requirements":["Validate attendee input and capacity.","Show inline errors without discarding form values.","Export a registration list using fictional data."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"web-development-p2","title":"Library lending system","brief":"Track books and loans through a browser and local service.","hours":12,"requirements":["Prevent duplicate active loans and support returns.","Persist records and provide search and pagination.","Test API contracts and database rollback behavior."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"web-development-p3","title":"Training center administration","brief":"Integrate courses, enrollments, and attendance in a local full-stack app.","hours":24,"requirements":["Separate domain, API, and persistence responsibilities.","Enforce capacity and role rules on the server.","Ship installation instructions, migration tests, and a local backup workflow."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Client: the program that asks for a resource.","Server: the program that receives and answers a request.","HTTP: a set of rules for web requests and responses.","Status: a number describing the result of a request."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nFirst complete Web Design, JavaScript, NodeJs, and ExpressJs. If these words are new, open the prerequisites below. This first example is a paper trace; it does not require a running server.","code":"Client: GET /books\nServer: 200 OK\nBody: [{\"title\":\"Algorithms\"}]","trace":["The client asks for the resource named /books.","The server reports 200 to indicate success.","The response body carries data. The browser still needs instructions to render that data on a page."],"question":"Which participant sends the response?","options":["The server","The CSS file","The keyboard"],"answer":0,"task":"Write a request and a successful response for a local /courses resource with one course.","hint":"Use the same three-part trace and change the resource and JSON data.","solution":"Client: GET /courses\nServer: 200 OK\nBody: [{\"title\":\"Python\"}]","result":"A request, a success status, and a JSON array containing one course are present."}},{"id":"java","title":"Java","prerequisites":[],"tools":"Install a JDK before going offline. Use javac Main.java and java Main for plain examples. Provision build tools and any test libraries beforehand; initial projects can use Java assertions with java -ea Main.","modules":[{"title":"Types and control flow","explanation":"Java checks declared types at compile time. Use integers for whole quantities and choose explicit rounding for money. A loop invariant states what remains true after every iteration.","example":"public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { int total = 0; for (int value : new int[]{10,20,30}) total += value; System.out.println(total); } }","practice":"a marks summary with bounds checks and a stated loop invariant.","plans":[{"id":"java-m1-l1","title":"Types and control flow: guided investigation","level":"remember","minutes":60,"objective":"Identify the key terms and syntax in types and control flow without consulting the example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a marks summary with bounds checks and a stated loop invariant. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."},{"id":"java-m1-l2","title":"Types and control flow: independent studio","level":"understand","minutes":90,"objective":"Explain how types and control flow changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"a marks summary with bounds checks and a stated loop invariant. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}]},{"title":"Classes and encapsulation","explanation":"Keep mutable fields private and expose operations that preserve invariants. Constructors establish valid state. An interface describes a capability without fixing its implementation.","example":"final class Stock { private int quantity; Stock(int q) { if(q < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); quantity=q; } int quantity() { return quantity; } }","practice":"a stock object that cannot be initialized with a negative quantity.","plans":[{"id":"java-m2-l1","title":"Classes and encapsulation: guided investigation","level":"understand","minutes":60,"objective":"Explain how classes and encapsulation changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a stock object that cannot be initialized with a negative quantity. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."},{"id":"java-m2-l2","title":"Classes and encapsulation: independent studio","level":"apply","minutes":90,"objective":"Implement a stock object that cannot be initialized with a negative quantity.","activity":"a stock object that cannot be initialized with a negative quantity. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}]},{"title":"Collections and generics","explanation":"List preserves order, Set expresses uniqueness, and Map associates keys with values. Generics reject incompatible element types during compilation. Define equality deliberately when objects become map keys.","example":"java.util.Map<String,Integer> stock = new java.util.HashMap<>();\nstock.put(\"Notebook\", 5);\nstock.merge(\"Notebook\", 2, Integer::sum);","practice":"a catalog with unique IDs, duplicate detection, and sorted reports.","plans":[{"id":"java-m3-l1","title":"Collections and generics: guided investigation","level":"apply","minutes":60,"objective":"Implement a catalog with unique IDs, duplicate detection, and sorted reports.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a catalog with unique IDs, duplicate detection, and sorted reports. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."},{"id":"java-m3-l2","title":"Collections and generics: independent studio","level":"analyze","minutes":90,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in collections and generics and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"a catalog with unique IDs, duplicate detection, and sorted reports. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}]},{"title":"Exceptions and file persistence","explanation":"A checked exception makes a failure path visible in an API. Try-with-resources closes owned resources. Separate malformed user data from unavailable storage so recovery messages are specific.","example":"try (var lines = java.nio.file.Files.lines(java.nio.file.Path.of(\"items.txt\"))) { lines.filter(s -> !s.isBlank()).forEach(System.out::println); }","practice":"a file importer that reports line numbers and preserves valid existing records.","plans":[{"id":"java-m4-l1","title":"Exceptions and file persistence: guided investigation","level":"analyze","minutes":60,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in exceptions and file persistence and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a file importer that reports line numbers and preserves valid existing records. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."},{"id":"java-m4-l2","title":"Exceptions and file persistence: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to exceptions and file persistence against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a file importer that reports line numbers and preserves valid existing records. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Streams, concurrency, and tests","explanation":"Streams express transformations but side effects make reasoning harder. Executors separate task submission from thread creation. Protect shared state or avoid it; test deterministic domain functions separately from scheduling.","example":"var names = java.util.List.of(\"Kabul\", \"Herat\", \"Bamyan\");\nvar sorted = names.stream().sorted().toList();\nassert sorted.size() == 3;","practice":"a report pipeline and compare sequential and concurrent results under repeated runs.","plans":[{"id":"java-m5-l1","title":"Streams, concurrency, and tests: guided investigation","level":"evaluate","minutes":60,"objective":"Judge two approaches to streams, concurrency, and tests against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a report pipeline and compare sequential and concurrent results under repeated runs. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."},{"id":"java-m5-l2","title":"Streams, concurrency, and tests: independent studio","level":"create","minutes":90,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using streams, concurrency, and tests with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"a report pipeline and compare sequential and concurrent results under repeated runs. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}]},{"title":"SOLID and delivery","explanation":"Dependency inversion lets business rules depend on a repository contract. A memory adapter supports fast tests; a file adapter supports persistence. Keep interfaces small and package a command-line entry point with reproducible execution instructions.","example":"interface ItemRepository { java.util.List<String> allNames(); }\nfinal class Report { private final ItemRepository repository; Report(ItemRepository r) { repository=r; } int count() { return repository.allNames().size(); } }","practice":"a repository-backed report with fake storage and failure-path tests.","plans":[{"id":"java-m6-l1","title":"SOLID and delivery: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using solid and delivery with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a repository-backed report with fake storage and failure-