, and a reasoned decision."}]}],"projects":[{"id":"java-p1","title":"Student gradebook","brief":"Produce course results from fictional marks.","hours":6,"requirements":["Validate marks from 0 to 100.","Calculate averages with a documented rounding rule.","Test empty classes and boundary marks."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"java-p2","title":"Library desktop core","brief":"Implement the domain and command-line interface of a library.","hours":12,"requirements":["Use stable IDs for books and members.","Reject borrowing an unavailable copy.","Persist to local files and report malformed records."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"java-p3","title":"Warehouse ledger","brief":"Maintain stock movements for a small warehouse.","hours":24,"requirements":["Encapsulate stock invariants and provide repository adapters.","Generate sorted reports and reconcile movement totals.","Test failure recovery and document the build and packaging steps."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Source file: a text file containing program instructions.","Compiler: a tool that checks and translates source code.","Method: a named group of instructions.","println: a method that displays a value followed by a new line."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nNo prior programming is required. A classroom computer needs an installed JDK. Save the example as Main.java. In that folder run javac Main.java, then java Main. Keep capital letters exactly as shown.","code":"public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(\"Salam\");\n  }\n}","trace":["Main is the class name and matches the file name. The outer braces enclose the class.","main is the entry method Java starts. Its braces enclose the instructions.","println displays Salam. The semicolon ends this statement. Do not delete the surrounding structure yet."],"question":"Which text is displayed by this program?","options":["Main.java","Salam","String[]"],"answer":1,"task":"Change the program so it displays Kabul instead of Salam.","hint":"Only change the characters inside the quoted string.","solution":"public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(\"Kabul\");\n  }\n}","result":"Kabul"}},{"id":"kotlin","title":"Kotlin","prerequisites":[],"tools":"Provision Kotlin/JVM and a JDK. Compile local code with kotlinc Main.kt -include-runtime -d main.jar, then java -jar main.jar. Coroutine libraries and Gradle dependencies, if used, must be cached before offline study.","modules":[{"title":"Values, expressions, and nullability","explanation":"Prefer val for bindings that do not change. A nullable type makes absence explicit; a safe call propagates null, while the Elvis operator supplies a fallback. Avoid force-unwrapping input that can be absent.","example":"fun main() { val name: String? = null; println(name?.uppercase() ?: \"Guest\") }","practice":"a greeting and fee calculator that handles missing optional input.","plans":[{"id":"kotlin-m1-l1","title":"Values, expressions, and nullability: guided investigation","level":"remember","minutes":60,"objective":"Identify the key terms and syntax in values, expressions, and nullability without consulting the example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a greeting and fee calculator that handles missing optional input. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."},{"id":"kotlin-m1-l2","title":"Values, expressions, and nullability: independent studio","level":"understand","minutes":90,"objective":"Explain how values, expressions, and nullability changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"a greeting and fee calculator that handles missing optional input. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}]},{"title":"Functions and collections","explanation":"Small functions can transform immutable lists. map changes each element, filter retains matching elements, and fold accumulates a result. Explain when a sequence avoids intermediate collections and when it adds overhead.","example":"val total = listOf(10, 20, 30).filter { it > 10 }.fold(0) { sum, n -> sum + n }","practice":"a purchase report using collection transformations and an empty-list test.","plans":[{"id":"kotlin-m2-l1","title":"Functions and collections: guided investigation","level":"understand","minutes":60,"objective":"Explain how functions and collections changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a purchase report using collection transformations and an empty-list test. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."},{"id":"kotlin-m2-l2","title":"Functions and collections: independent studio","level":"apply","minutes":90,"objective":"Implement a purchase report using collection transformations and an empty-list test.","activity":"a purchase report using collection transformations and an empty-list test. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}]},{"title":"Data classes and sealed results","explanation":"A data class represents values; a sealed hierarchy enumerates alternatives. A when expression can handle each result explicitly, making missing cases visible when the model changes.","example":"sealed interface SaveResult\ndata class Saved(val id: Int) : SaveResult\ndata class Rejected(val reason: String) : SaveResult","practice":"a validation API that returns a typed success or failure instead of ambiguous null.","plans":[{"id":"kotlin-m3-l1","title":"Data classes and sealed results: guided investigation","level":"apply","minutes":60,"objective":"Implement a validation API that returns a typed success or failure instead of ambiguous null.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a validation API that returns a typed success or failure instead of ambiguous null. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."},{"id":"kotlin-m3-l2","title":"Data classes and sealed results: independent studio","level":"analyze","minutes":90,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in data classes and sealed results and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"a validation API that returns a typed success or failure instead of ambiguous null. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}]},{"title":"Extensions, interfaces, and generics","explanation":"Extensions add convenient syntax without changing a type’s actual members. Interfaces define replaceable behavior. Keep transformations separate from storage and pass dependencies to constructors for testability.","example":"interface Repository<T> { fun all(): List<T> }\nfun String.normalizedName(): String = trim().lowercase()","practice":"a generic repository and duplicate-name detection with explicit normalization rules.","plans":[{"id":"kotlin-m4-l1","title":"Extensions, interfaces, and generics: guided investigation","level":"analyze","minutes":60,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in extensions, interfaces, and generics and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a generic repository and duplicate-name detection with explicit normalization rules. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."},{"id":"kotlin-m4-l2","title":"Extensions, interfaces, and generics: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to extensions, interfaces, and generics against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a generic repository and duplicate-name detection with explicit normalization rules. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Coroutines and structured concurrency","explanation":"Suspending is not the same as blocking a thread. A coroutine scope owns child work and cancellation. Dispatch blocking IO appropriately and propagate cancellation; do not swallow it inside a broad failure handler.","example":"// Requires a provisioned kotlinx-coroutines dependency.\nsuspend fun loadNames(repository: NameRepository): List<String> = repository.load()\ninterface NameRepository { suspend fun load(): List<String> }","practice":"a cancellable report operation with deterministic fake-repository tests.","plans":[{"id":"kotlin-m5-l1","title":"Coroutines and structured concurrency: guided investigation","level":"evaluate","minutes":60,"objective":"Judge two approaches to coroutines and structured concurrency aga