ecific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.","plans":[{"id":"kotlin-m16-l1","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using capstone delivery and maintenance with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."},{"id":"kotlin-m16-l2","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to capstone delivery and maintenance against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]}],"projects":[{"id":"kotlin-p1","title":"Study planner","brief":"Organize weekly study tasks.","hours":6,"requirements":["Model optional deadlines safely.","Filter tasks by course and completion.","Test empty and duplicate task input."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"kotlin-p2","title":"Expense categorizer","brief":"Summarize local expense records.","hours":12,"requirements":["Use typed validation results and immutable models.","Import a local file with useful errors.","Generate category totals and test malformed records."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"kotlin-p3","title":"Offline inventory core","brief":"Create a reusable Kotlin inventory library and CLI.","hours":24,"requirements":["Expose repository contracts and typed operations.","Support cancellation for long reports with provisioned coroutine tools.","Deliver tests, local persistence, and JVM execution instructions."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Function: a named group of instructions.","main: the starting function of this example.","val: a binding that cannot be reassigned.","String: a text value enclosed in quotes."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nNo prior programming is required. On a provisioned computer save Main.kt. Compile with kotlinc Main.kt -include-runtime -d main.jar, then run java -jar main.jar. The app cannot run Kotlin code.","code":"fun main() {\n  val city = \"Kabul\"\n  println(city)\n}","trace":["fun introduces a function. The braces surround its body.","val gives the text Kabul a name, city.","println reads city and displays Kabul. It does not print the word city."],"question":"What is the value referred to by city?","options":["println","Kabul","main"],"answer":1,"task":"Use a val named greeting to display Salam.","hint":"Change the binding name and the quoted value, then update println.","solution":"fun main() {\n  val greeting = \"Salam\"\n  println(greeting)\n}","result":"Salam"}},{"id":"android","title":"Android","prerequisites":["kotlin"],"tools":"Provision Android Studio, JDK, Android SDK, emulator image or device drivers, and all Gradle/Compose/Room dependencies. Use Gradle offline mode after provisioning. The learning app cannot compile Android projects itself.","modules":[{"title":"Android project and lifecycle","explanation":"An Activity is a platform entry point, not a permanent store. Configuration changes can recreate it. Identify manifest declarations, resources, build variants, and the difference between app process lifetime and screen lifetime.","example":"class MainActivity : androidx.activity.ComponentActivity() {\n override fun onCreate(state: android.os.Bundle?) {\n  super.onCreate(state)\n  setContent { androidx.compose.material3.Text(\"Salam\") }\n }\n}\n// In a Compose project, import androidx.activity.compose.setContent.","practice":"a local Android screen and describe what survives rotation and process recreation.","plans":[{"id":"android-m1-l1","title":"Android project and lifecycle: guided investigation","level":"remember","minutes":60,"objective":"Identify the key terms and syntax in android project and lifecycle without consulting the example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a local Android screen and describe what survives rotation and process recreation. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."},{"id":"android-m1-l2","title":"Android project and lifecycle: independent studio","level":"understand","minutes":90,"objective":"Explain how android project and lifecycle changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"a local Android screen and describe what survives rotation and process recreation. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}]},{"title":"Compose state and layouts","explanation":"Composable functions describe UI from state. Hoist state when multiple components need one source of truth. remember holds state across recomposition; persistent and restorable state require a deliberate owner.","example":"@Composable\nfun Counter() {\n var count by rememberSaveable { mutableIntStateOf(0) }\n Button(onClick = { count++ }) { Text(\"Count: $count\") }\n}\n// Import Compose runtime, saveable, and Material3 symbols.","practice":"a counter and form that preserve appropriate fields after rotation.","plans":[{"id":"android-m2-l1","title":"Compose state and layouts: guided investigation","level":"understand","minutes":60,"objective":"Explain how compose state and layouts changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a counter and form that preserve appropriate fields after rotation. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."},{"id":"android-m2-l2","title":"Compose state and layouts: independent studio","level":"apply","minutes":90,"objective":"Implement a counter and form that preserve appropriate fields after rotation.","activity":"a counter and form that preserve appropriate fields after rotation. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}]},{"title":"Navigation and ViewModel","explanation":"Navigation identifies destinations and arguments; a ViewModel owns screen logic. Expose UI state and accept user actions. Pass stable IDs between destinations instead of serializing mutable database entities.","example":"data class UiState(val names: List<String> = emptyList(), val error: String? = null)\n// A ViewModel exposes StateFlow<UiState>; the UI collects with lifecycle awareness.","practice":"a list-to-detail flow with loading, empty, content, and error states.","plans":[{"id":"android-m3-l1","title":"Navigation and ViewModel: guided investigation","level":"apply","minutes":60,"objective":"Implement a list-to-detail flow with loading, empty, content, and error states.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a list-to-detail flow with loading, empty, content, and error states. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."},{"id":"android-m3-l2","title":"Navigation and ViewModel: independent studio","level":"analyze","minutes":90,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in navigation and viewmodel and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"a list-to-detail flow with loading, empty, content, and error states. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}]},{"title":"Room and repository boundaries","explanation":"Room maps structured data to SQLite. Entities express storage, DAOs express queries, and repositories expose application operations. Use migrations when schemas change and keep writes off the UI thread.","example":"@Entity\ndata class Note(@PrimaryKey val id: Long, val text: String)\n@Dao\ninterface Notes { @Query(\"SELECT * FROM Note ORDER BY id\") fun observe(): Flow<List<Note>> }\n// Requires Room and Flow imports and provisioned code generation.","practice":"a note repository with a migration test and a restart persistence check.","plans":[{"id":"android-m4-l1","title":"Room and repository boundaries: guided investigation","level":"analyze","minutes":60,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in room and repository boundaries and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a note repository with a migration test and a restart persistence check. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."},{"id":"android-m4-l2","title":"Room and repository boundaries: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to room and repository boundaries against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a note repository with a migration test and a restart persistence check. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Background work and permissions","explanation":"Request only capabilities needed for an explicit feature. Long deferred work and immediate screen work have different owners. A background task may retry, so its effects must be idempotent. A local-only app needs no internet permission.","example":"// Background export state: queued -> running -> succeeded | failed.\n// Store an operation ID; a repeated export must not duplicate ledger entries.","practice":"an export workflow that reports cancellation and retries without duplicating data.","plans":[{"id":"android-m5-l1","title":"Background work and permissions: guided investigation","level":"evaluate","minutes":60,"objective":"Judge two approaches to background work and permissions against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. an export workflow that reports cancellation and retries without duplicating data. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."},{"id":"android-m5-l2","title":"Background work and permissions: independent studio","level":"create","minutes":90,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using background work and permissions with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"an export workflow that reports cancellation and retries without duplicating data. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}]},{"title":"Accessibility, testing, and release","explanation":"Test domain rules, Compose interactions, and device lifecycle behavior separately. Provide labels and adequate touch targets. An unsigned or debug build is not a production release; document signing ownership and test an installed build in airplane mode.","example":"@Test fun emptyNameIsRejected() { assertFalse(validateName(\"\")) }\nfun validateName(name: String) = name.isNotBlank()","practice":"an accessible release candidate with lifecycle tests and a documented offline installation path.","plans":[{"id":"android-m6-l1","title":"Accessibility, testing, and release: guided investigation","level":"cr