e limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance","explanation":"This iOS module teaches you to plan incremental delivery, version data safely, document offline setup, review risks, and maintain the product after its first release. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possible failure. Then test a normal case, an empty or boundary case, and an invalid case. Record the actual results instead of claiming that the design works.\n\nHint: begin with one input and one expected result. Make that case work, add the failure case, and only then extend the design. Keep the smallest solution you can explain and reproduce.","example":"Release evidence:\n- acceptance checks pass\n- migration and rollback tested\n- offline install documented\n- known limits published\n- next improvement prioritized","practice":"Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.","plans":[{"id":"ios-m16-l1","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using capstone delivery and maintenance with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths,