ts, and limitations."]},{"id":"ios-p2","title":"Reading journal","brief":"Track books and reading notes.","hours":12,"requirements":["Persist Codable documents atomically.","Support edit, search, and safe delete confirmation.","Test corrupted files and app relaunch."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"ios-p3","title":"Workshop organizer","brief":"Manage fictional workshops and attendance locally.","hours":24,"requirements":["Enforce capacities through domain logic.","Provide accessible list, detail, and attendance flows.","Use repository tests and demonstrate simulator operation offline."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Scene: one instance of an app interface.","View: a description of visible interface content.","SwiftUI: Apple’s declarative interface framework.","Simulator: a tool for running a simulated device on a Mac."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nComplete Swift first. Use a compatible Mac with provisioned Xcode. Create an iOS SwiftUI app. Replace the generated ContentView with this example and run the prepared simulator. This cannot be compiled on an Android phone.","code":"import SwiftUI\nstruct ContentView: View {\n  var body: some View {\n    Text(\"Salam\")\n  }\n}","trace":["The import makes SwiftUI types available.","ContentView conforms to View and supplies a body.","The body describes one text element. Xcode’s generated app entry point displays ContentView."],"question":"What is displayed by the view?","options":["Salam","ContentView.swift","import SwiftUI"],"answer":0,"task":"Display Welcome using the same view structure.","hint":"Change only the string inside Text.","solution":"import SwiftUI\nstruct ContentView: View {\n  var body: some View { Text(\"Welcome\") }\n}","result":"The simulator displays Welcome."}},{"id":"swift","title":"Swift","prerequisites":[],"tools":"Provision a supported Swift toolchain. Use swift main.swift for basic programs and swift test for package tests. Foundation availability varies by platform; iOS UI work belongs to the iOS course and requires Xcode on macOS.","modules":[{"title":"Values, control flow, and optionals","explanation":"Swift distinguishes absent values with Optional. Use conditional binding or guard to handle absence. let prevents rebinding, while value types help avoid accidental shared mutation.","example":"let input = \"42\"\nif let amount = Int(input) { print(amount + 1) } else { print(\"Invalid amount\") }","practice":"an integer parser with explicit invalid-input behavior.","plans":[{"id":"swift-m1-l1","title":"Values, control flow, and optionals: guided investigation","level":"remember","minutes":60,"objective":"Identify the key terms and syntax in values, control flow, and optionals without consulting the example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. an integer parser with explicit invalid-input behavior. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."},{"id":"swift-m1-l2","title":"Values, control flow, and optionals: independent studio","level":"understand","minutes":90,"objective":"Explain how values, control flow, and optionals changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"an integer parser with explicit invalid-input behavior. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}]},{"title":"Functions and collections","explanation":"Functions state parameter and return types. Arrays preserve order, dictionaries map keys, and sets express uniqueness. Separate transformations from IO and prefer readable intermediate names over dense chains.","example":"func total(_ values: [Int]) -> Int { values.reduce(0, +) }\nassert(total([]) == 0)","practice":"a grade summary with empty and boundary cases.","plans":[{"id":"swift-m2-l1","title":"Functions and collections: guided investigation","level":"understand","minutes":60,"objective":"Explain how functions and collections changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a grade summary with empty and boundary cases. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."},{"id":"swift-m2-l2","title":"Functions and collections: independent studio","level":"apply","minutes":90,"objective":"Implement a grade summary with empty and boundary cases.","activity":"a grade summary with empty and boundary cases. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}]},{"title":"Structs, enums, and protocols","explanation":"Structs have value semantics; classes share reference identity. Enums can carry associated values to model outcomes. Protocols express capabilities and let code depend on behavior instead of a concrete implementation.","example":"enum Validation { case accepted(Int); case rejected(String) }\nstruct Item { let id: Int; var quantity: Int }","practice":"a stock validation result and compare copied structs with shared class instances.","plans":[{"id":"swift-m3-l1","title":"Structs, enums, and protocols: guided investigation","level":"apply","minutes":60,"objective":"Implement a stock validation result and compare copied structs with shared class instances.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a stock validation result and compare copied structs with shared class instances. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."},{"id":"swift-m3-l2","title":"Structs, enums, and protocols: independent studio","level":"analyze","minutes":90,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in structs, enums, and protocols and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"a stock validation result and compare copied structs with shared class instances. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}]},{"title":"Errors, Codable, and generics","explanation":"Throwing functions make failure explicit. Codable handles representation, not domain validity. Generic algorithms share behavior across types while retaining compiler checks. Validate decoded values before accepting them.","example":"import Foundation\nstruct Expense: Codable { let amount: Int }\nlet bytes = try JSONEncoder().encode(Expense(amount: 20))\nprint(try JSONDecoder().decode(Expense.self, from: bytes).amount)","practice":"a JSON import that distinguishes malformed bytes from invalid amounts.","plans":[{"id":"swift-m4-l1","title":"Errors, Codable, and generics: guided investigation","level":"analyze","minutes":60,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in errors, codable, and generics and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a JSON import that distinguishes malformed bytes from invalid amounts. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."},{"id":"swift-m4-l2","title":"Errors, Codable, and generics: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to errors, codable, and generics against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a JSON import that distinguishes malformed bytes from invalid amounts. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"ARC and concurrency","explanation":"Automatic reference counting manages object lifetimes, but strong reference cycles can keep objects alive. Actors isolate mutable state; async does not guarantee parallel work. Choose ownership before adding weak references or detached tasks.","example":"actor Counter { private var value = 0; func increment() { value += 1 }; func read() -> Int { value } }","practice":"an actor-based counter and an ownership diagram for a callback retaining its owner.","plans":[{"id":"swift-m5-l1","title":"ARC and concurrency: guided investigation","level":"evaluate","minutes":60,"objective":"Judge two approaches to arc and concurrency against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. an actor-based counter and an ownership diagram for a callback retaining its owner. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."},{"id":"swift-m5-l2","title":"ARC and concurrency: independent studio","level":"create","minutes":90,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using arc and concurrency with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"an actor-based counter and an ownership diagram for a callback retaining its owner. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}]},{"title":"Packages, tests, and design","explanation":"Swift packages organize modules and tests. Hide file access behind a protocol, keep domain models independent, and document public contracts. Compare alternative abstractions with a concrete change request instead of counting patterns.","example":"protocol LedgerStore { func amounts() throws -> [Int] }\nfunc balance(_ store: LedgerStore) throws -> Int { try store.amounts().reduce(0, +) }","practice":"a package with a fake store, tested failure paths, and a local usage example.","plans":[{"id":"swift-m6-l1","title":"Packages, tests, and design: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using packages, tests, and design with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a package with a fake store, tested failure paths, and a local usage example. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."},{"id":"swift-m6-l2","title":"Packages, tests, and design: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to packages, tests, and design against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a package with a fake store, tested failure paths, and a local usage example. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Actors and advanced concurrency","explanation":"This Swift module teaches you to use actors, task groups, cancellation, Sendable values, and isolation boundaries safely. Use the idea in the course project or in a small offline records feature. Read the example line by line and identify its input, rule, result, and possibl