cceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"cpp-p3","title":"Warehouse stock engine","brief":"Build a C++ inventory core and CLI.","hours":24,"requirements":["Use RAII and clear ownership contracts.","Prevent invalid stock transitions and preserve files after errors.","Deliver modular source, boundary tests, and profiling notes."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Header: a declaration file included before compilation.","main: the function where this program begins.","Stream: a sequence of input or output data.","Statement: one instruction, often ending with a semicolon."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nNo prior programming is required. Use a provisioned C++ compiler. Save main.cpp and compile with g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra main.cpp -o app. Run ./app, or app.exe on Windows. The app itself cannot compile C++.","code":"#include <iostream>\nint main() {\n  std::cout << \"Salam\" << \"\\n\";\n  return 0;\n}","trace":["iostream declares standard input and output facilities.","main contains the program statements between braces.","cout sends Salam and a newline to the output. return 0 reports successful completion to the operating system."],"question":"Which expression sends text to standard output?","options":["std::cout","#include","return 0"],"answer":0,"task":"Display Kabul followed by a newline.","hint":"Replace Salam inside the quotes; keep the stream operators and semicolon.","solution":"#include <iostream>\nint main() {\n  std::cout << \"Kabul\" << \"\\n\";\n  return 0;\n}","result":"Kabul"}},{"id":"javascript","title":"JavaScript","prerequisites":["web-design"],"tools":"Use a browser’s developer tools for pure language examples and local HTML with classic scripts. ES modules and fetch may require a provisioned local server. Node.js is optional for node --test; no remote scripts are needed.","modules":[{"title":"Values, scope, and control flow","explanation":"const prevents rebinding; it does not freeze an object. Use strict equality to avoid implicit coercion surprises. Block scope limits where a binding exists. Distinguish undefined from a deliberately absent null.","example":"const prices = [10, 20, 30];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const price of prices) total += price;\nconsole.log(total);","practice":"a budget calculation with explicit numeric conversion and invalid-input handling.","plans":[{"id":"javascript-m1-l1","title":"Values, scope, and control flow: guided investigation","level":"remember","minutes":60,"objective":"Identify the key terms and syntax in values, scope, and control flow without consulting the example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a budget calculation with explicit numeric conversion and invalid-input handling. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."},{"id":"javascript-m1-l2","title":"Values, scope, and control flow: independent studio","level":"understand","minutes":90,"objective":"Explain how values, scope, and control flow changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"a budget calculation with explicit numeric conversion and invalid-input handling. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}]},{"title":"Functions, arrays, and objects","explanation":"Functions can receive other functions. Array methods express transformations; avoid mutating shared input when a calculation can return a new value. Object spread copies only one level, so nested objects may still be shared.","example":"const expenses = [{category:\"books\", amount:20}, {category:\"travel\", amount:10}];\nconst total = expenses.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.amount, 0);","practice":"a category report without mutating the original expense array.","plans":[{"id":"javascript-m2-l1","title":"Functions, arrays, and objects: guided investigation","level":"understand","minutes":60,"objective":"Explain how functions, arrays, and objects changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a category report without mutating the original expense array. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."},{"id":"javascript-m2-l2","title":"Functions, arrays, and objects: independent studio","level":"apply","minutes":90,"objective":"Implement a category report without mutating the original expense array.","activity":"a category report without mutating the original expense array. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}]},{"title":"DOM, events, and forms","explanation":"The DOM is a mutable document tree. Respond to user events, validate input, then render from your data model. Use textContent for user text; inserting untrusted HTML creates an injection boundary.","example":"const button = document.querySelector(\"button\");\nbutton.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { document.querySelector(\"output\").textContent = \"Saved locally\"; });","practice":"an accessible task form that rejects blank input and renders text safely.","plans":[{"id":"javascript-m3-l1","title":"DOM, events, and forms: guided investigation","level":"apply","minutes":60,"objective":"Implement an accessible task form that rejects blank input and renders text safely.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. an accessible task form that rejects blank input and renders text safely. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."},{"id":"javascript-m3-l2","title":"DOM, events, and forms: independent studio","level":"analyze","minutes":90,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in dom, events, and forms and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"an accessible task form that rejects blank input and renders text safely. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}]},{"title":"Promises and the event loop","explanation":"Promises represent eventual results. await pauses the async function, not the whole browser. Handle rejection and stale responses; the last request started may not be the last one completed.","example":"async function load(read) { try { return await read(); } catch { return []; } }\nload(() => Promise.resolve([\"Algorithms\"])).then(console.log);","practice":"a simulated asynchronous search that discards outdated results.","plans":[{"id":"javascript-m4-l1","title":"Promises and the event loop: guided investigation","level":"analyze","minutes":60,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in promises and the event loop and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a simulated asynchronous search that discards outdated results. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."},{"id":"javascript-m4-l2","title":"Promises and the event loop: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to promises and the event loop against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a simulated asynchronous search that discards outdated results. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Modules and browser storage","explanation":"Modules define explicit dependencies. Browser storage is local but can be cleared or unavailable. JSON decoding needs schema checks; store a version and offer an export before changing a format.","example":"const snapshot = {version:1, tasks:[{id:1, title:\"Study\"}]};\nlocalStorage.setItem(\"tasks\", JSON.stringify(snapshot));","practice":"a versioned task store that handles malformed JSON without replacing good data.","plans":[{"id":"javascript-m5-l1","title":"Modules and browser storage: guided investigation","level":"evaluate","minutes":60,"objective":"Judge two approaches to modules and browser storage against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a versioned task store that handles malformed JSON without replacing good data. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."},{"id":"javascript-m5-l2","title":"Modules and browser storage: independent studio","level":"create","minutes":90,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using modules and browser storage with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"a versioned task store that handles malformed JSON without replacing good data. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."}]},{"title":"Testing and maintainable design","explanation":"Keep domain functions independent of the DOM so edge cases are easy to test. Test events and storage separately through adapters. A passing sample is insufficient: include empty input, duplicates, and failures