","Persist a versioned snapshot and handle corrupted data.","Export and import a validated JSON backup."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"javascript-p3","title":"Local quiz studio","brief":"Author and take quizzes without a service.","hours":24,"requirements":["Separate question data, scoring, and rendering.","Support keyboard interaction and accessible feedback.","Test scoring boundaries, persistence, and malformed imports."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Script: instructions executed by a JavaScript runtime.","const: a binding that cannot be reassigned.","Console: a developer tool that displays messages.","Expression: code that produces a value."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nBegin with Web Design so you can create local files. Open a browser’s developer tools and its Console. Enter the two lines below. No website account or internet service is needed.","code":"const amount = 20;\nconsole.log(amount + 5);","trace":["const gives the number 20 the name amount.","The addition expression calculates 25 before console.log displays it.","The semicolon ends a statement. Numbers are not quoted here; quoted values would be strings."],"question":"What number does the example display?","options":["20","5","25"],"answer":2,"task":"Store 30 in price and display price plus 10.","hint":"Use a numeric value without quotes and pass the addition expression to console.log.","solution":"const price = 30;\nconsole.log(price + 10);","result":"40"}},{"id":"reactjs","title":"ReactJs","prerequisites":["javascript"],"tools":"Provision Node.js and a React project with pinned dependencies and a populated package cache. Use local development/build scripts from that project. All data, fonts, and images must be local; do not depend on a CDN.","modules":[{"title":"Components, JSX, and props","explanation":"Components describe reusable UI. Props are inputs owned by a parent; do not mutate them. Break a design into components by responsibility and identify the minimum data required for each one.","example":"function CourseCard({title}) { return <article><h2>{title}</h2></article>; }","practice":"a component tree for a course catalog with explicit props.","plans":[{"id":"reactjs-m1-l1","title":"Components, JSX, and props: guided investigation","level":"remember","minutes":60,"objective":"Identify the key terms and syntax in components, jsx, and props without consulting the example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a component tree for a course catalog with explicit props. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit five correctly defined terms and label their occurrences in the example; correct at least four before continuing."},{"id":"reactjs-m1-l2","title":"Components, JSX, and props: independent studio","level":"understand","minutes":90,"objective":"Explain how components, jsx, and props changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"a component tree for a course catalog with explicit props. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."}]},{"title":"State, events, and controlled inputs","explanation":"State drives rendering. Use a state update function when the next value depends on the previous one. Controlled inputs keep the current value in state; validate without losing what the user typed.","example":"function Counter() { const [count,setCount] = useState(0); return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c+1)}>{count}</button>; }\n// import {useState} from \"react\";","practice":"a controlled expense form with validation and a derived total.","plans":[{"id":"reactjs-m2-l1","title":"State, events, and controlled inputs: guided investigation","level":"understand","minutes":60,"objective":"Explain how state, events, and controlled inputs changes program behavior using a traced example.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a controlled expense form with validation and a derived total. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit a state trace and a causal explanation of two outputs. Explain a changed input without running it first."},{"id":"reactjs-m2-l2","title":"State, events, and controlled inputs: independent studio","level":"apply","minutes":90,"objective":"Implement a controlled expense form with validation and a derived total.","activity":"a controlled expense form with validation and a derived total. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."}]},{"title":"Lists, keys, and reducers","explanation":"Keys preserve identity between renders; array positions are unstable when rows move. A reducer centralizes state transitions and can reject invalid actions. Derived values usually do not need separate state.","example":"function reducer(state, action) { if(action.type === \"add\") return [...state, action.item]; return state; }","practice":"a task reducer and test add, edit, delete, and duplicate-ID actions.","plans":[{"id":"reactjs-m3-l1","title":"Lists, keys, and reducers: guided investigation","level":"apply","minutes":60,"objective":"Implement a task reducer and test add, edit, delete, and duplicate-ID actions.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a task reducer and test add, edit, delete, and duplicate-ID actions. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit source and normal, empty, and invalid input results. Each stated acceptance condition must pass."},{"id":"reactjs-m3-l2","title":"Lists, keys, and reducers: independent studio","level":"analyze","minutes":90,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in lists, keys, and reducers and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"a task reducer and test add, edit, delete, and duplicate-ID actions. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."}]},{"title":"Effects and external systems","explanation":"Effects synchronize with systems outside rendering, such as storage or subscriptions. Clean up subscriptions and avoid using effects for values that can be calculated during render. Treat asynchronous completion after unmount as a lifecycle concern.","example":"useEffect(() => { const handler = () => setOnline(navigator.onLine); window.addEventListener(\"online\",handler); return () => window.removeEventListener(\"online\",handler); }, []);","practice":"a storage adapter and explain which updates require an effect and which are derived.","plans":[{"id":"reactjs-m4-l1","title":"Effects and external systems: guided investigation","level":"analyze","minutes":60,"objective":"Locate a failing assumption in effects and external systems and isolate it with a minimal reproduction.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. a storage adapter and explain which updates require an effect and which are derived. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit the failing case, root cause, minimal fix, and a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after."},{"id":"reactjs-m4-l2","title":"Effects and external systems: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to effects and external systems against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"a storage adapter and explain which updates require an effect and which are derived. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]},{"title":"Composition, accessibility, and tests","explanation":"Composition often avoids 