ractice":"Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback.","plans":[{"id":"mongodb-m16-l1","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: guided investigation","level":"create","minutes":60,"objective":"Design and deliver an original extension using capstone delivery and maintenance with explicit acceptance tests.","activity":"Read the explanation. Trace the worked example on paper, record each state change, then run it using the course toolchain. Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Compare the actual result with your prediction.","evidence":"Submit your own requirements, design, source, test evidence, and a short demonstration. Explain how your extension differs from the example."},{"id":"mongodb-m16-l2","title":"Capstone delivery and maintenance: independent studio","level":"evaluate","minutes":90,"objective":"Judge two approaches to capstone delivery and maintenance against correctness, maintainability, and offline operation.","activity":"Deliver a course-specific capstone increment with requirements, architecture decision, implementation evidence, tests, usability review, migration or rollback plan, and a short demonstration. Use fictional data, explain each important step in your own words, test normal and failure paths, and record one improvement you would make after feedback. Change one requirement, implement your own solution, then deliberately introduce an edge-case failure. Diagnose and repair it without copying the worked example.","evidence":"Submit a comparison of two designs, evidence from tests, one limitation of the chosen design, and a reasoned decision."}]}],"projects":[{"id":"mongodb-p1","title":"Learning resource catalog","brief":"Store fictional learning resources with tags and searchable titles.","hours":6,"requirements":["Validate required fields and stable identifiers.","Compare embedding and referencing for lesson metadata.","Demonstrate tag filters and deterministic result ordering."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"mongodb-p2","title":"Document expense reports","brief":"Aggregate fictional expenses and keep invalid records out.","hours":12,"requirements":["Validate amount types and nonnegative values.","Trace every stage of a grouped report.","Test empty categories and repeated fixture setup."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]},{"id":"mongodb-p3","title":"Local document recovery lab","brief":"Practice consistent local backup and restore with fictional collections.","hours":24,"requirements":["Restore to a separate target namespace.","Compare document counts and report totals.","Document standalone and replica-set transaction differences."],"milestones":["Discover: write three user stories, scope exclusions, and acceptance examples using fictional data.","Design: sketch components and data flow; justify one tradeoff in an architecture decision record.","Build: implement the required behaviors in small reviewable changes; record how to reproduce the build offline.","Verify: test every acceptance condition, empty/invalid data, and restart behavior; retain results.","Review: demonstrate to a peer or conduct a recorded self-review; document feedback, improvements, and limitations."]}],"beginner":{"terms":["Database: organized information that a program can read and change.","Record: one stored item, represented by a row or a document.","Query: a request describing which information you want.","Constraint: a rule that rejects invalid stored data."],"before":"This is the true starting point for this course. No vocabulary from this course is assumed. You will first learn what the example is for, the meaning of its four key terms, and how to follow it one step at a time. Read the prerequisite course shown in the catalog first when one is required.\n\nA document stores named fields together, while a collection groups documents. Think of one library book with a title and a list of tags. MongoDB stores BSON documents; the shell uses JavaScript-like notation to express them. Flexible fields do not mean that a program can safely accept any shape.","code":"db.books.insertOne({_id:1,title:\"Python\",tags:[\"coding\",\"beginner\"]});\ndb.books.find({_id:1},{_id:0,title:1,tags:1});","trace":["insertOne creates one document with a fixed training id. find matches that id and the projection hides _id while returning title and tags. Repeating the insert unchanged fails on the duplicate id. Use a fresh training collection rather than silently overwriting someone else's data."],"question":"What should you do before changing training data?","options":["Predict the result and identify the target records.","Assume every command changes only one row.","Use the learner progress database for experiments."],"answer":0,"task":"Create a second book with a distinct id and find only its title.","hint":"Use a fresh fictional fixture. Read the worked trace and change only the requested fields or query.","solution":"db.books.insertOne({_id:2,title:\"Databases\",tags:[\"data\"]});\ndb.books.find({_id:2},{_id:0,title:1});","result":"One result document contains title: Databases. The first book is unchanged."}}]''';
